1. What tools do you currently use for group work?
We use either WhatsApp or Instagram, mainly Instagram because it is more universally accessible and used. Google docs is also commonly used for collaborative work.

2. How do you usually communicate with your group members?
We mostly communicate through group chats on Instagram or try to have meetings in person. Group chats are good for progress updates and clearing up confusions or conflicts with the group work. However, private messages are more useful when your progress directly depends on someone else's. Too many direct messages in a group chat can clutter a group chat and impede on group progression.

3. What difficulties do you face when working in a group?
Communicating can be difficult when face to face with other people who don't enjoy talking. Group coordination can also be difficult if someone doesn't meet their responsibilities. Langauge barriers can be difficult. When working with AI, some will output different conclusions from the same prompts, resulting in a clash of information. When group members don't have the same level of standard, that can also be difficult. The main difficulty is ensuring everyone has even responsibilities and are meeting them in a timely manner as well. 

4. Can you describe a situation where group work did not go well?
Some members didn't meet the same standard of work, meaning that during the final presentation, it was very evident that some members had detailed research and were able to speak on their respective parts but others weren't knowledgeable in their sections. It made the project overall seem disorganised. There were also communication errors and delays during the group work. 

5. How are tasks assigned in your group? Is this effective?
It usually starts with one proactive person claiming some tasks for themselves, then people slowly allocating themselves their own tasks and balancing them all together as a group. The task distribution is typically fair in this manner as people get what they want and the weight of the responsibilities are balanced as a group. It typically is effective in motivating people to accomplish the tasks they want and reassuring that everyone has similar work loads. The only real issues come in people not following up with their assigned tasks. 

6. Have you experienced communication issues? How did they affect your work?
Yes, sometimes too many individual or conversations between 2-3 people in a group chat can clutter the group and important details may be covered up. This requires others to be filled in later and confusions can occur. Also, sometimes a language barrier can disrupt effective group communication, making it dry or misunderstandings can occur.

7. What features would improve your group work experience?
Timestamps or completion markers to track individual or group progression. Chat boxes to use while working. Visual or attention grabbing stimuli. Some form of a task board with weighted tasks using points or a similar system. AI checking system. Timetabling for meetings or other activities.
